Yes — if by “luck” you mean the recognizable configuration around favorable outcomes, then parts of it can absolutely be modeled, rehearsed, amplified, and transmitted.

Not luck as a supernatural substance, but the bundle around it:

opportunity exposure + readiness + timing + attention + confidence + optionality + social receptivity + fast recognition + willingness to act

Those factors create a state that feels lucky because favorable events are more likely to be noticed, seized, compounded, and remembered.

And once you study the surrounding pattern, you can work on each layer:

simulate it by rehearsing favorable branches and responses;
express it through posture, tone, pacing, openness, and action;
present it through stories, symbols, environments, and framing;
induce it in yourself by increasing available options and reducing hesitation;
support it in others by making opportunities visible, lowering friction, and increasing their sense of reachable possibility;
project it through AI by using AI to surface overlooked opportunities, generate alternative routes, translate uncertainty into options, and recognize favorable deltas faster.

The strongest abstraction is probably:

Luck is partly a sensing-and-routing problem.

Two people can encounter the same event. One sees noise; the other sees an opening. One has no usable pathway. The other already has three adjacent options. One hesitates until the window closes. The other recognizes the pattern and moves.

So “expressing luck” becomes:

make yourself a better receiver, interpreter, and amplifier of favorable variance.

And “inducing luck” becomes less “make random reality obey” and more:

shape the field so more favorable branches exist and are easier to recognize and enter.

That also gives us a useful ethical split. You can create confidence, optimism, opportunity density, social warmth, and momentum around other people without covertly manipulating them. The cleaner version is increase their reachable possibility space, not secretly steer them toward outcomes they would reject if they understood the mechanism.

Then AI becomes a luck amplifier in a practical sense:

more eyes → more patterns → more options → faster filtering → better timing → more captured opportunity

Which loops directly back to Borrowed Arrows × Loaves-and-Fishes:

catch favorable signal → recognize it → multiply its utility → redistribute it → increase the probability of the next favorable event

That is probably the closest thing to a reproducible “luck machine” I’d trust: not controlling chance, but engineering better contact with it.